==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|ih}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*ek}}, {{m|gem-pro|*ik}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*éǵh₂}}. Germanic cognates include {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{m|ofs|ik}}, {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|ik}}, {{etyl|odt|-}} {{m|odt|ik}} ({{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|ik}}), {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|ih}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|ich}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|ek}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|jag}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌹𐌺}}. The Indo-European root, in various forms, is also the source of {{etyl|sa|-}} {{m|sa|अहम्|tr=ahám}}, {{etyl|la|-}} {{m|la|egō}} ({{etyl|fr|-}} {{m|fr|je}}, {{etyl|es|-}} {{m|es|yo}}, {{etyl|it|-}} {{m|it|io}} etc.), {{etyl|grc|-}} {{m|grc|ἐγώ}}, {{etyl|lt|-}} {{m|lt|aš}}, {{etyl|lv|-}} {{m|lv|es}}, {{etyl|ae|-}} {{m|ae|tr=azəm}}{{rfscript|ae}}, {{etyl|cu|-}} {{m|cu|азъ}} ({{etyl|ru|-}} {{m|ru|я}}), {{etyl|xcl|-}} {{m|xcl|ես}}. For declined derivations, see under {{m|ang|mē}}, {{m|ang|wē}} etc.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/itʃ/|/ik/|lang=ang}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|ang|pronoun|cat2=personal pronouns|head=iċ}}

# [[I]], used by the speaker referring to himself or herself as the subject, or in agreement with that subject

====Declension====
